(27792,44,'Acts','NT',24,26,'He hoped also that money should have been given him of Paul, that he might loose him: wherefore he sent for him the oftener, and communed with him.'),
(27793,44,'Acts','NT',24,27,'But after two years Porcius Festus came into Felix\' room: and Felix, willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, left Paul bound.'),
(27794,44,'Acts','NT',25,1,'Now when Festus was come into the province, after three days he ascended from Caesarea to Jerusalem.'),
(27795,44,'Acts','NT',25,2,'Then the high priest and the chief of the Jews informed him against Paul, and besought him,'),
(27796,44,'Acts','NT',25,3,'And desired favour against him, that he would send for him to Jerusalem, laying wait in the way to kill him.'),
(27797,44,'Acts','NT',25,4,'But Festus answered, that Paul should be kept at Caesarea, and that he himself would depart shortly {thither}.'),
(27798,44,'Acts','NT',25,5,'Let them therefore, said he, which among you are able, go down with {me}, and accuse this man, if there be any wickedness in him.'),
(27799,44,'Acts','NT',25,6,'And when he had tarried among them more than ten days, he went down unto Caesarea; and the next day sitting on the judgment seat commanded Paul to be brought. {more...: or, as some copies read, no more than eight or ten days}'),
(27800,44,'Acts','NT',25,7,'And when he was come, the Jews which came down from Jerusalem stood round about, and laid many and grievous complaints against Paul, which they could not prove.'),
(27801,44,'Acts','NT',25,8,'While he answered for himself, Neither against the law of the Jews, neither against the temple, nor yet against Caesar, have I offended any thing at all.'),
(27802,44,'Acts','NT',25,9,'But Festus, willing to do the Jews a pleasure, answered Paul, and said, Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things before me?'),
(27803,44,'Acts','NT',25,10,'Then said Paul, I stand at Caesar\'s judgment seat, where I ought to be judged: to the Jews have I done no wrong, as thou very well knowest.'),
(27804,44,'Acts','NT',25,11,'For if I be an offender, or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die: but if there be none of these things whereof these accuse me, no man may deliver me unto them. I appeal unto Caesar.'),
(27805,44,'Acts','NT',25,12,'Then Festus, when he had conferred with the council, answered, Hast thou appealed unto Caesar? unto Caesar shalt thou go.'),
(27806,44,'Acts','NT',25,13,'And after certain days king Agrippa and Bernice came unto Caesarea to salute Festus.'),
(27807,44,'Acts','NT',25,14,'And when they had been there many days, Festus declared Paul\'s cause unto the king, saying, There is a certain man left in bonds by Felix:'),
