(28776,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,3,'And when I come, whomsoever ye shall approve by {your} letters, them will I send to bring your liberality unto Jerusalem. {liberality: Gr. gift}'),
(28777,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,4,'And if it be meet that I go also, they shall go with me.'),
(28778,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,5,'Now I will come unto you, when I shall pass through Macedonia: for I do pass through Macedonia.'),
(28779,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,6,'And it may be that I will abide, yea, and winter with you, that ye may bring me on my journey whithersoever I go.'),
(28780,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,7,'For I will not see you now by the way; but I trust to tarry a while with you, if the Lord permit.'),
(28781,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,8,'But I will tarry at Ephesus until Pentecost.'),
(28782,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,9,'For a great door and effectual is opened unto me, and {there are} many adversaries.'),
(28783,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,10,'Now if Timotheus come, see that he may be with you without fear: for he worketh the work of the Lord, as I also {do}.'),
(28784,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,11,'Let no man therefore despise him: but conduct him forth in peace, that he may come unto me: for I look for him with the brethren.'),
(28785,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,12,'As touching {our} brother Apollos, I greatly desired him to come unto you with the brethren: but his will was not at all to come at this time; but he will come when he shall have convenient time.'),
(28786,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,13,'Watch ye, stand fast in the faith, quit you like men, be strong.'),
(28787,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,14,'Let all your things be done with charity.'),
(28788,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,15,'I beseech you, brethren, (ye know the house of Stephanas, that it is the firstfruits of Achaia, and {that} they have addicted themselves to the ministry of the saints,)'),
(28789,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,16,'That ye submit yourselves unto such, and to every one that helpeth with {us}, and laboureth.'),
(28790,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,17,'I am glad of the coming of Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus: for that which was lacking on your part they have supplied.'),
(28791,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,18,'For they have refreshed my spirit and yours: therefore acknowledge ye them that are such.'),
(28792,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,19,'The churches of Asia salute you. Aquila and Priscilla salute you much in the Lord, with the church that is in their house.'),
(28793,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,20,'All the brethren greet you. Greet ye one another with an holy kiss.'),
(28794,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,21,'The salutation of {me} Paul with mine own hand.'),
(28795,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,22,'If any man love not the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be Anathema Maranatha.'),
(28796,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,23,'The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ {be} with you.'),
(28797,46,'1 Corinthians','NT',16,24,'My love {be} with you all in Christ Jesus. Amen. «{The first epistle} to the Corinthians was written from Philippi by Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus and Timotheus.}»'),
(28798,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,1,'Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy {our} brother, unto the church of God which is at Corinth, with all the saints which are in all Achaia:'),
(28799,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,2,'Grace {be} to you and peace from God our Father, and {from} the Lord Jesus Christ.'),
(28800,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,3,'Blessed {be} God, even the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies, and the God of all comfort;'),
(28801,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,4,'Who comforteth us in all our tribulation, that we may be able to comfort them which are in any trouble, by the comfort wherewith we ourselves are comforted of God.'),
(28802,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,5,'For as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so our consolation also aboundeth by Christ.'),
(28803,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,6,'And whether we be afflicted, {it is} for your consolation and salvation, which is effectual in the enduring of the same sufferings which we also suffer: or whether we be comforted, {it is} for your consolation and salvation. {is effectual: or, is wrought}'),
(28804,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,7,'And our hope of you {is} stedfast, knowing, that as ye are partakers of the sufferings, so {shall ye be} also of the consolation.'),
(28805,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,8,'For we would not, brethren, have you ignorant of our trouble which came to us in Asia, that we were pressed out of measure, above strength, insomuch that we despaired even of life:'),
(28806,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,9,'But we had the sentence of death in ourselves, that we should not trust in ourselves, but in God which raiseth the dead: {sentence: or, answer}'),
(28807,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,10,'Who delivered us from so great a death, and doth deliver: in whom we trust that he will yet deliver {us};'),
(28808,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,11,'Ye also helping together by prayer for us, that for the gift {bestowed} upon us by the means of many persons thanks may be given by many on our behalf.'),
(28809,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,12,'For our rejoicing is this, the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity and godly sincerity, not with fleshly wisdom, but by the grace of God, we have had our conversation in the world, and more abundantly to you-ward.'),
(28810,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,13,'For we write none other things unto you, than what ye read or acknowledge; and I trust ye shall acknowledge even to the end;'),
(28811,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,14,'As also ye have acknowledged us in part, that we are your rejoicing, even as ye also {are} ours in the day of the Lord Jesus.'),
(28812,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,15,'And in this confidence I was minded to come unto you before, that ye might have a second benefit; {benefit: or, grace}'),
(28813,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,16,'And to pass by you into Macedonia, and to come again out of Macedonia unto you, and of you to be brought on my way toward Judaea.'),
(28814,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,17,'When I therefore was thus minded, did I use lightness? or the things that I purpose, do I purpose according to the flesh, that with me there should be yea yea, and nay nay?'),
(28815,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,18,'But {as} God {is} true, our word toward you was not yea and nay. {word: or, preaching}'),
(28816,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,19,'For the Son of God, Jesus Christ, who was preached among you by us, {even} by me and Silvanus and Timotheus, was not yea and nay, but in him was yea.'),
(28817,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,20,'For all the promises of God in him {are} yea, and in him Amen, unto the glory of God by us.'),
(28818,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,21,'Now he which stablisheth us with you in Christ, and hath anointed us, {is} God;'),
(28819,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,22,'Who hath also sealed us, and given the earnest of the Spirit in our hearts.'),
(28820,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,23,'Moreover I call God for a record upon my soul, that to spare you I came not as yet unto Corinth.'),
(28821,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',1,24,'Not for that we have dominion over your faith, but are helpers of your joy: for by faith ye stand.'),
(28822,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,1,'But I determined this with myself, that I would not come again to you in heaviness.'),
(28823,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,2,'For if I make you sorry, who is he then that maketh me glad, but the same which is made sorry by me?'),
(28824,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,3,'And I wrote this same unto you, lest, when I came, I should have sorrow from them of whom I ought to rejoice; having confidence in you all, that my joy is {the joy} of you all.'),
(28825,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,4,'For out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote unto you with many tears; not that ye should be grieved, but that ye might know the love which I have more abundantly unto you.'),
(28826,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,5,'But if any have caused grief, he hath not grieved me, but in part: that I may not overcharge you all.'),
(28827,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,6,'Sufficient to such a man {is} this punishment, which {was inflicted} of many. {punishment: or, censure}'),
(28828,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,7,'So that contrariwise ye {ought} rather to forgive {him}, and comfort {him}, lest perhaps such a one should be swallowed up with overmuch sorrow.'),
(28829,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,8,'Wherefore I beseech you that ye would confirm {your} love toward him.'),
(28830,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,9,'For to this end also did I write, that I might know the proof of you, whether ye be obedient in all things.'),
(28831,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,10,'To whom ye forgive any thing, I {forgive} also: for if I forgave any thing, to whom I forgave {it}, for your sakes {forgave I it} in the person of Christ; {person: or, sight}'),
(28832,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,11,'Lest Satan should get an advantage of us: for we are not ignorant of his devices.'),
(28833,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,12,'Furthermore, when I came to Troas to {preach} Christ\'s gospel, and a door was opened unto me of the Lord,'),
(28834,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,13,'I had no rest in my spirit, because I found not Titus my brother: but taking my leave of them, I went from thence into Macedonia.'),
(28835,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,14,'Now thanks {be} unto God, which always causeth us to triumph in Christ, and maketh manifest the savour of his knowledge by us in every place.'),
(28836,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,15,'For we are unto God a sweet savour of Christ, in them that are saved, and in them that perish:'),
(28837,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,16,'To the one {we are} the savour of death unto death; and to the other the savour of life unto life. And who {is} sufficient for these things?'),
(28838,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',2,17,'For we are not as many, which corrupt the word of God: but as of sincerity, but as of God, in the sight of God speak we in Christ. {corrupt: or, deal deceitfully with} {in Christ: or, of Christ}'),
(28839,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,1,'Do we begin again to commend ourselves? or need we, as some {others}, epistles of commendation to you, or {letters} of commendation from you?'),
(28840,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,2,'Ye are our epistle written in our hearts, known and read of all men:'),
(28841,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,3,'{Forasmuch as ye are} manifestly declared to be the epistle of Christ ministered by us, written not with ink, but with the Spirit of the living God; not in tables of stone, but in fleshy tables of the heart.'),
(28842,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,4,'And such trust have we through Christ to God-ward:'),
(28843,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,5,'Not that we are sufficient of ourselves to think any thing as of ourselves; but our sufficiency {is} of God;'),
(28844,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,6,'Who also hath made us able ministers of the new testament; not of the letter, but of the spirit: for the letter killeth, but the spirit giveth life. {giveth life: or, quickeneth}'),
(28845,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,7,'But if the ministration of death, written {and} engraven in stones, was glorious, so that the children of Israel could not stedfastly behold the face of Moses for the glory of his countenance; which {glory} was to be done away:'),
(28846,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,8,'How shall not the ministration of the spirit be rather glorious?'),
(28847,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,9,'For if the ministration of condemnation {be} glory, much more doth the ministration of righteousness exceed in glory.'),
(28848,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,10,'For even that which was made glorious had no glory in this respect, by reason of the glory that excelleth.'),
(28849,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,11,'For if that which is done away {was} glorious, much more that which remaineth {is} glorious.'),
(28850,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,12,'Seeing then that we have such hope, we use great plainness of speech: {plainness: or, boldness}'),
(28851,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,13,'And not as Moses, {which} put a vail over his face, that the children of Israel could not stedfastly look to the end of that which is abolished:'),
(28852,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,14,'But their minds were blinded: for until this day remaineth the same vail untaken away in the reading of the old testament; which {vail} is done away in Christ.'),
(28853,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,15,'But even unto this day, when Moses is read, the vail is upon their heart.'),
(28854,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,16,'Nevertheless when it shall turn to the Lord, the vail shall be taken away.'),
(28855,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,17,'Now the Lord is that Spirit: and where the Spirit of the Lord {is}, there {is} liberty.'),
(28856,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',3,18,'But we all, with open face beholding as in a glass the glory of the Lord, are changed into the same image from glory to glory, {even} as by the Spirit of the Lord. {by the...: or, of the Lord the Spirit}'),
(28857,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',4,1,'Therefore seeing we have this ministry, as we have received mercy, we faint not;'),
(28858,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',4,2,'But have renounced the hidden things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness, nor handling the word of God deceitfully; but by manifestation of the truth commending ourselves to every man\'s conscience in the sight of God. {dishonesty: Gr. shame}'),
(28859,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',4,3,'But if our gospel be hid, it is hid to them that are lost:'),
(28860,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',4,4,'In whom the god of this world hath blinded the minds of them which believe not, lest the light of the glorious gospel of Christ, who is the image of God, should shine unto them.'),
(28861,47,'2 Corinthians','NT',4,5,'For we preach not ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord; and ourselves your servants for Jesus\' sake.'),
